ghtening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Stephanie Bice (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Stephanie Bice seeks a third term in an Oklahoma City–area district that leans Republican by about nine points. Her challenger, Jena Nelson, won a competitive Democratic primary and has drawn attention from national Democrats, an unusual investment in Oklahoma. Bice has declined debates so far but holds a commanding fundraising position, with roughly $2 million on hand compared with about $17,000 for Nelson, and has highlighted federal project funding for the district.\n\nRaters overwhelmingly call the race Solid or Safe Republican, though a few say Likely R, and prediction markets give Nelson only a small chance. A single Democratic-sponsored poll from late September showed Bice ahead by five points, closer than the district's lean would suggest. The forecast expects Bice to win by around ten points, with Nelson's path requiring a late surge in money and attention.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0558, "margin": -10.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0638, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1454, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0909, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1152, "margin": -7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0488, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.099, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1151, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0881, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0678, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0911, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0833, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.097, "margin": -8.8}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.0712, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.0816, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.0827, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.0573, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_de